Understood. Let's strip away the complexity and build the **Minimum Viable Hypermedia** version.

We are assuming a "High Trust" environment.
*   **Identity:** Passed via simple query param (`?as_user=thmorriss`).
*   **State:** Carried in URL parameters.
*   **Writes:** Performed via GET requests (e.g., clicking a link commits a vote).

Here is the structural plan to make Sorter "AI-Browsable" immediately.

### 1. The Great Purge (Cleanup)
We need to stop thinking in JSON.
1.  **Delete `frontsorter/`**: It is gone.
2.  **Modify `rustsorter/Cargo.toml`**:
    *   Add `maud = { version = "*", features = ["axum"] }`. (Maud is the fastest HTML templating engine for Rust; it writes HTML as Rust macros).
3.  **Route Cleanup in `main.rs`**:
    *   Comment out or delete the API routes returning `Json<T>`.
    *   We are building a flat router that returns `maud::Markup`.

### 2. The "System Prompt" Landing Page (`GET /`)
When you paste `https://sorter.social/?as_user=thmorriss` into Claude, the response needs to be an instruction manual for the model.

**The Logic:**
*   Accept `as_user` query param.
*   Check if user exists (if not, maybe auto-create or error).
*   Render the "Root" of the graph.

**The Structure (Pseudo-Maud):**
```rust
html! {
    (DOCTYPE)
    html {
        head { title { "Sorter OS" } }
        body {
            h1 { "Welcome to Sorter, AI Agent." }
            
            // INSTRUCTIONS FOR THE AI
            section id="system-instructions" {
                p { "You are acting on behalf of user: " (user_handle) }
                p { "Your goal is to traverse this graph to find consensus." }
                p { "To ingest content, follow 'Ingest' links." }
                p { "To vote, present 'Compare' links to the user." }
            }

            // ENTRY POINTS
            h2 { "Entry Points" }
            ul {
                li { a href=(format!("/explore/recent?as_user={}", user_handle)) { "Explore Recent Entities" } }
                li { a href=(format!("/ranking/global?as_user={}", user_handle)) { "Global Rankings" } }
                li { a href=(format!("/me?as_user={}", user_handle)) { "My Profile & Stats" } }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### 3. The Entity Node (`GET /view/:ns/:pk`)
This replaces the JSON response. It needs to provide the AI with *just enough* info to decide what to do next.

**The Content:**
1.  **Identity:** Title, Image (as an `<img>` tag so Claude sees it), Metadata.
2.  **Graph Edges:**
    *   **Parents:** (e.g., if this is a Song, link to Album).
    *   **Children:** (e.g., if this is a Playlist, link to Songs).
3.  **Actions:**
    *   "Find Similar"
    *   "Compare This" (The core mechanism).

**The AI Logic:**
If the user asks "What is the best song on this album?", the AI will:
1.  Fetch Album Page.
2.  See list of Children (Songs).
3.  Click "View Rankings for Children".

### 4. The "Comparator" (`GET /pair`)
This is the most important page. It serves two masters:
1.  **The AI:** Needs to know *what* is being compared to summarize it.
2.  **The User:** Needs clickable links to cast the vote.

**The Response HTML:**
```html
<article class="comparison">
    <h1>Comparison: A vs B</h1>
    
    <!-- Left Entity Data -->
    <section id="left">
        <h2>Bohemian Rhapsody</h2>
        <img src="..." />
    </section>

    <!-- Right Entity Data -->
    <section id="right">
        <h2>Hey Jude</h2>
        <img src="..." />
    </section>

    <!-- The Interaction Mechanism -->
    <!-- Since we are doing "No Security", these are just GET links that write to DB -->
    <section id="voting-booth">
        <h3>Cast Vote:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><a href="/vote/commit?winner=left&mag=50&...">Much Better (Left)</a></li>
            <li><a href="/vote/commit?winner=left&mag=10&...">Slightly Better (Left)</a></li>
            <li><a href="/vote/skip?...">Skip / IDK</a></li>
            <li><a href="/vote/commit?winner=right&mag=10&...">Slightly Better (Right)</a></li>
            <li><a href="/vote/commit?winner=right&mag=50&...">Much Better (Right)</a></li>
        </ul>
    </section>
</article>
```

**The UX Flow:**
1.  User: "Help me sort my heavy metal songs."
2.  Claude: Fetches list, finds songs.
3.  Claude (Output): "I've found the songs. Here is a comparison for you to decide:"
    *   **[Vote: Master of Puppets >>> Iron Man]**
    *   **[Vote: Master of Puppets > Iron Man]**
    *   **[Vote: Iron Man > Master of Puppets]**
    *   **[Vote: Iron Man >>> Master of Puppets]**
4.  User: Clicks a link.
5.  Browser: Hits `GET /vote/commit`.
6.  Server: Writes vote, **Redirects** to a new `/pair` (infinite loop of sorting).

### 5. The "Ingestor" (`GET /ingest`)
Currently, you rely on Spotify OAuth imports. For an AI agent, "Ingestion" is just finding a link.

**Workflow:**
1.  User: "Add https://open.spotify.com/track/123 to the pool."
2.  Claude: Recognizes it can't write directly.
3.  Claude: Constructs a URL: `https://sorter.social/ingest?url=...&as_user=...`
4.  Claude: Fetches it.
5.  **Server:**
    *   Detects Spotify URL.
    *   Fetches Metadata (server-side).
    *   Creates Entity.
    *   Returns HTML: "Created 'Song Title'. <a href='/view/...'>View it here</a>."

### 6. Immediate Implementation Steps
To verify this works without refactoring the entire DB layer yet:

1.  **Create `src/views.rs`:** A file to hold your Maud templates.
2.  **Create a "Harness" in `main.rs`:**
    *   Keep your existing JSON API at `/api/...` (for now).
    *   Mount the new HTML interface at `/` (root).
3.  **Implement `GET /`:** Just a hardcoded HTML page using `maud` that links to one specific Entity you know exists in your DB (e.g., the "admin" user or a specific song).
4.  **Test with Claude:** Paste your local URL (via ngrok/cloudflared) into Claude and say "Explore this."

This gets you exploring the graph *today*. Security and writes come later.
